 "executive\_summary": "The 61-paper corpus supports a modular picture rather than a validated unified controller. Structured sufficiency and gap representations can drive iterative retrieval and early stopping on multi-hop QA, while adaptive retrieval studies show that additional retrieval can be beneficial, neutral, or harmful depending on the query and evidence state. Separate conformal and selective-prediction work provides calibrated risk or coverage control, including several forms of distribution-shift adaptation. Temporal and version-aware systems improve retrieval over evolving documents, and separate permission-aware RAG systems enforce access constraints. Budget-aware controllers reduce calls, tokens, or latency in several benchmark settings. However, no admitted study combines structured evidence gaps, temporal/version lineage, permission-aware replenishment, calibrated stopping under shift, abstention, and total-cost control in one evaluated iterative retrieval loop. All eight prior gaps therefore remain open.",
